I think Dean’s response was kind and helpful.  Mine is more blunt.  Why are you calling a landmark a module?

There will always be problems in R when you ask analyses intended for matrices (or arrays that can be coerced to matrices) to work for vectors.  Programmers can try to avoid the pitfalls, but in this case, attempting to make sure an analysis works with one-landmark modules is awkward.

Here is a demo for how R thinks

> x <- matrix(1:3, 1, 3)
> x
     [,1] [,2] [,3]
[1,]    1    2    3
> as.matrix(x)
     [,1] [,2] [,3]
[1,]    1    2    3
> y <- array(x, c(1,3,1))
> y
, , 1

     [,1] [,2] [,3]
[1,]    1    2    3

> as.matrix(y)
     [,1]
[1,]    1
[2,]    2
[3,]    3
>

R has to try to interpret how to coerce an array into a matrix when the array is a vector, and as a default, it makes vectors into single-column matrices (not rows).  It would be possible to build code into two.b.pls (or similar functions)  to do a different form of data manipulation in the one case a user decides to define a module as a single landmark but that would require the programmer to foresee that as a meaningful thing to do (which I can’t).  But as I said in a recent thread, it is probably wise for the user to have some skill with data formatting.  The two.b.pls function can handle matrix input; the user can make sure that data are properly coerced into matrices before using it.  Maybe this is something for which we can build a trap and fix the problem, but understanding data formatting goes a long way in avoiding pitfalls, which are inevitable with any package that attempts to build on base R.

Regardless, the better question might be what is the sense of a single landmark as a module?  It is difficult to understand the purpose of that.

Best,
Mike

