Thank you Prof. Dean and Dr. Mike for the careful explorarion. My intention is to creat an Anatomical Imbalance Map (AIM) per-person as insired by the PNAS paper mentioned early in this thread. More specifically, the (i, j)'s entry of a person's AIM matrix should represent the magnitude of this individual's deviation from the norm of association between landmark i and j.

I wish to establish the individual-level AIM matrix in two steps:
1. Obtain PLS1 scores for landmark i and j, respectively through PLS analysis (This is the reason why I am considering using one single landmark per module);
2. Perform regression of the left and right PLS1 scores and calculate orthogonal residuals. Each residual corresponds to the (i, j)'s entry in one person's AIM matrix, reflecting how the association between the i_th and j_th landmark of this individual deviates from the norm, as obtained through regression.

After obtaining this individual-level AIM matrix, I wish to follow the PNAS paper's method to investigate changes in AIM matrix with age.

While I am aware of the recent post about the inappropriateness of analysis of landmarks one at a time after GPA, it seems to me that I am not doing something like performing regression separately for each landmark and identifying genetic variants associated with a particular landmark. But I am far from confident in claiming my approach to be correct. I am indeed curious to learn if the above approach to construction of AIM matrix for morphometric data is appropriate, esp in terms of the appropriateness of single landmark PLS in the above context.

Kieran

On Tue, Jun 15, 2021, 3:47 AM Mike Collyer <mlco...@gmail.com> wrote:

> I think Dean’s response was kind and helpful.  Mine is more blunt.  Why are you calling a landmark a module?
>
> There will always be problems in R when you ask analyses intended for matrices (or arrays that can be coerced to matrices) to work for vectors.  Programmers can try to avoid the pitfalls, but in this case, attempting to make sure an analysis works with one-landmark modules is awkward.
>
> Here is a demo for how R thinks
>
> > x <- matrix(1:3, 1, 3)
> > x
>      [,1] [,2] [,3]
> [1,]    1    2    3
> > as.matrix(x)
>      [,1] [,2] [,3]
> [1,]    1    2    3
> > y <- array(x, c(1,3,1))
> > y
> , , 1
>
>      [,1] [,2] [,3]
> [1,]    1    2    3
>
> > as.matrix(y)
>      [,1]
> [1,]    1
> [2,]    2
> [3,]    3
> >
>
> R has to try to interpret how to coerce an array into a matrix when the array is a vector, and as a default, it makes vectors into single-column matrices (not rows).  It would be possible to build code into two.b.pls (or similar functions)  to do a different form of data manipulation in the one case a user decides to define a module as a single landmark but that would require the programmer to foresee that as a meaningful thing to do (which I can’t).  But as I said in a recent thread, it is probably wise for the user to have some skill with data formatting.  The two.b.pls function can handle matrix input; the user can make sure that data are properly coerced into matrices before using it.  Maybe this is something for which we can build a trap and fix the problem, but understanding data formatting goes a long way in avoiding pitfalls, which are inevitable with any package that attempts to build on base R.
>
> Regardless, the better question might be what is the sense of a single landmark as a module?  It is difficult to understand the purpose of that.
>
> Best,
> Mike
>
> > On Jun 14, 2021, at 2:57 PM, Adams, Dean [EEOB] <dca...@iastate.edu> wrote:
> >
> > No, there is technically not a lower limit on the number of variables. This is an input data dimensionality problem. I will need to consider the extent to which this is a ‘user error’ issue or a bug; as what you tried in your ‘plsb’ example was not anticipated by the geomorph team.  I explain below, along with the solution.
> >
> > First, what you did in that example was call a single landmark in each group variables. That is the unanticipated part: one does not typically do such an operation, as it often leads to nonsensical results.  One reason is that landmark-by-landmark interpretations of shape changes are discouraged due to the GPA step (see last week’s Morphmet thread where this came up in another context).  Your example
> >
> > Now, the consequence of this for your example was that the objects being shunted into two.b.pls were 2 x 30, and not 1 x 2 x 30.  Thus, the dimensions were not consistent from your first to second example. That lead to the incorrect result.
> >
> > If you simply do the following, your second example will work correctly:
> >
> > plsb <- two.b.pls(t(proc$rotated[1,,]),t(proc$rotated[5,,])) # 1 landmark per module
> >
> > NOTE: see that I added ‘t’ before each component. That turns these matrices into transposed matrices, so that each specimen is a row with 2 columns of output.  Now the analysis works as intended.
> >
> > I will have to ponder, and consult with the team, as to whether or not this is in fact a bug, or something we need to warn people about.  But regardless, the fix above will cause the analysis to work properly for your special situation.
> >
> > Best,
