“While I am aware of the recent post about the inappropriateness of analysis of landmarks one at a time after GPA, it seems to me that I am not doing something like performing regression separately for each landmark and identifying genetic variants associated with a particular landmark”

Both regression and PLS – the two analytical tools your pipeline includes – operate on variation and covariation. Fine to use for entire structures, but when GPA-aligned landmarks are reduced to single landmarks for input into these analysis, one is, in fact, proposing a procedure that circles back to the original problem of interpreting variation landmark by landmark.

I would suggest some alternative to what you are attempting. I am not sure how one would use this procedure, and then post-hoc eliminate the GPA-induced pattern from the biological pattern.

This was not a problem with the original PNAS paper as it used different data. I think it is a problem here, when attempted on shape data.

Dean

