Thank you Prof. Dean for the reply. The original paper used cortical thickness, rather than morphometric data, to quantify morphology. So no problem for the paper. Here is one alternative idea that I may wish to learn about your opinion.

In a recent study by Profico Antonio, Piras Paolo et al. (Arothron: An R package for geometric morphometric methods and virtual anthropology applications), the authors developed the localmeshdiff fuction in their Arothron package to compute and visualize local differences between two surface meshes defined by the same topology (same triangulation of vertices). More specifically, local deformation is quantified as the percentage difference in area of each facet between two meshes. The right and left tails of the distribution of surface area differences indicate local expansion and contraction, respectively.

Enlighted by Profico's study, I may wish to calculate the area of each facet for each specimen after GPA. Then the surface area of the facets could be used in the same way cortical thickness data are used for the creation of AIM matrix. Since the localmeshdiff fuction is specifically designed to examine local shape differences, it seems to be free from the pitfalls of landmark-by-landmark analysis. I would like to learn about your expert opinion on this alternative workflow. Thank you.

Regards,
Kieran

